    \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u044b\u0439 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0436\u0438\u043b \u0441\u0432\u043e\u044e
    \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u044c \u043f\u043e \u0441\u043e\u0431\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u043d\u044b\u043c,
    \u043e\u0447\u0435\u043d\u044c \u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0433\u0438\u043c \u043f\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0438\u043b\u0430\u043c."
  interestingFactsEn: "Godfrey Harold Hardy's life is a treasure of fancy, unusual habits, and amazing stories. He was a man who made eccentricity a real art. Here's the most detailed collection of interesting facts about \"the king of pure mathematics.\"\n\n♪ Pathological hatred of his own appearance is perhaps the most famous of his weirdness. Hardy was almost manic aversion to his reflection. War with mirrors: When he entered any hotel room or new room, he was the first one to systematically throw towels, headscarves, or any tissue on all mirrors, without exception. He could not stand the appearance of his own face. Photos: This hatred was extended to photographs.He was avoiding the lens with such persistence that there were only five amateur photographs of Hardy around the world, and no official studios. 2.\n\nHe refused to visit church services on official grounds, but his atheism was not a boring denial, but a fun intellectual game. He invented a complex bet system with Heavens: A journey from Denmark: After visiting colleagues in Copenhagen, before leaving the ship, Hardy sent a telegram to England saying, \"I have proven the Roman hypothesis.\"He said, \"O my Lord, if there is a God, He will not let the ship sink.\"He said, \"If there is a God and he wants to prevent him from working, He will certainly send down rain so that Hardy can stay at home and sit at the table.\n\nThe second life, the cricket of Mathematics was his first and main passion, but the cricket held a strong second place. Mathematician sportsman: Hardy was not just a fan, he himself played cricket on a rather decent level. He was a great field player and batsman and continued to play in university matches even at a fairly mature age. Expert: He knew the statistics and rules of the game thoroughly.He often said to his friends (including Keynes and Littlewood) that there were only two real pleasures in life: to do pure mathematics and watch a good cricket. Schedule: Planning his academic work, he always checked with the calendar of cricket matches. Summer semesters were less productive in terms of mathematics, because he spent all his free time at the Lords stadium 4.\n\n♪ Destiny's letter and 12 points ♪ The history of Ramanujan's discovery is full of dramatism. Hardy received a letter from an Indian clerk with a crow of formulas. Many of them were incorrect or poorly written. But Hardy did not throw the letter away. Together with Littlewood, they began to dissect it and stumbled upon one formula concerning endless rows. Hardy later admitted that one look at the formula was enough to understand the author's genius.He has not seen such formulas since the time of Eyler and Jacoby, and Hardy has valued Ramanujan's talent \"on a scale of 0 to 100 to 12 points\" (he claimed that Gauss would have received 100, Eiler 90 and Ramanujan 100, but because of lack of education 50). He later said that his greatest contribution to mathematics was the discovery of Ramanujan.\n\n~ Taxi and the number 1729 is the most famous bike about Hardy and Ramanujan. On one occasion, Hardy visited the patient Ramanujan at the sanatorium in Patney. By driving a taxi, Hardy pointed out that the car number was very boring — 1729. He entered the room and told Ramanujan: \"I think it's a rather boring number.\"To which Ramanujan, who had a phenomenal intuition about numbers, responded instantly: \"No, Hardy, this is a very interesting number! This is the smallest number that can be expressed as the sum of two cubes in two different ways.\" That is, \\((1x3 + 12x3 = 1729 )\\(9x3 + 10x3 = 1729 ) Since then, the number of 1729 in mathematics has been known as \"the number Hardy-Ramanujan\" (or taxi number).\n\nAs a suicide letter, Hardy wrote his famous book, Math Apology, in 1940, when he was 62 years old. He saw his old age and his math ability as a painful acuteity. He thought that maths were getting older than other scientists, and their best work was young.He wrote this book as a farewell letter, believing that it would be his last word before he left life (although he lived for seven more years), which is why the book is so deeply pessimistic and melancholy that he compares himself to an ageing athlete who can no longer show the best result.\n\nThe pedantism in Hardy's schedule was obsessed with the routine of the day. He never wore a watch, but he knew the exact time of his body and his inner senses. They say he could tell a colleague, \"Now it's exactly 3:15,\" and the clock confirmed it. He never used a phone as an invasion of personal space.All of his correspondence he kept exclusively through letters and notes which he transmitted through servants or colleagues. 8.\n\n♪ Hardy's fundamental uselessness was proud that his math was completely useless for military and industrial needs. ♪ When German mathematician Ludwig Prandtle began working for military aviation in the First World War, Hardy was terrified. ♪ He was happy that the number theory was not applicable in ballistics ♪The ultimate blow to him was that during the Second World War, his favorite \"clean\" number theory began to be applied to cryptography (the Enigma Code hack). Although he himself had no direct connection to it, the very fact that its abstract structures had benefited the military had led him to despair.\n\nHis students Hardy was a brilliant teacher, but he did it on his own terms. He hated to run standard exams (Tripos), considering them \"rat hunting\", but he was the one who reformed the teaching system to make it more rigorous. Among his students was Mary Cartwright, one of the first women mathematicians to gain world recognition, who later became the first woman to be president of the London Mathematical Society.Hardy treated her with great respect, even though women mathematicians were rare in those years. 10.\n\n* \"Apology\" is the bestseller Malo who knows that \"Mathematics Apology\" has become a bestseller not among mathematicians, but among literary and philosophical writers. This book is a model of the 20th century English prose. She was praised for style, irony, and honesty. Hardy himself, being modest, would be surprised that his philosophy was read by people far from the integrals and rows.This collection of facts shows that Hardy was not just a \"dry scientist\" but a deeply passionate, ironic and extremely unusual man who lived his life by his own very strict rules."
